Wait, let me re-read the fuzz target:
```c
char Buffer[Size + 1];
std::memcpy(Buffer, Data, Size);
Buffer[Size] = 0;
char demangled[4096];
google::Demangle(Buffer, demangled, Size);
```

The buffer is `Size+1` bytes, null-terminated. So reads within the buffer are fine. But the issue is a **dynamic-stack-buffer-overflow READ 1**. This means reading 1 byte past the end of the `Buffer` array.

Let me look at `AtLeastNumCharsRemaining`:
```c
static bool AtLeastNumCharsRemaining(const char *str, ssize_t n) {
  for (ssize_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    if (str[i] == '\0') {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

This reads `str[i]` for `i` from 0 to n-1. If `n` is large and the string is short, it reads up to the null terminator and stops. That's fine.

But wait - `ParseNumber` reads `*p != '\0'` in a loop. If the input is all digits with no null terminator... but the buffer IS null-terminated at `Buffer[Size]`.

